# self-fix-boundary — what devs may fix without prior coordinator approval

> Devs occasionally see a related-but-out-of-scope problem mid-task. This file says when to fix-it-now vs handoff-and-wait.

## The rule

Stay strictly within task-md scope **unless** the change satisfies all three:

1. **Trivial** — < ~20 lines, no logic change, no architectural touch
2. **Safe** — no risk of regression (no shared state, no API surface)
3. **In allowlist** — the change category appears in §allowlist below

If any of the three is questionable → **don't fix**, list under `§ escalation 候选` in the handoff.

## §allowlist (auto-fix OK)

These are pre-approved as low-risk. Devs may include them in the same commit as the main task. Mention each in the handoff `§ 改动文件清单` and a one-liner under `§ verify`.

| Category | Examples | Boundary |
|---|---|---|
| **Typo in already-touched file** | `viweModel` → `viewModel`; commit msg typo | Only in files you modified for the main task |
| **Import sort / unused import** | sorting; removing unused imports IDE flagged | Same as above |
| **Lint-fix in changed lines** | trailing whitespace; missing newline at EOF; var instead of val | Only on lines you already touched |
| **Stale TODO referencing your work** | TODO comment about the feature you just implemented | Remove or update to reflect new state |
| **Stale doc comment** | KDoc/JavaDoc/docstring lying about behaviour you just changed | Update to match new behaviour |
| **Test fixture matching new API** | test expects old return type; you changed it | Update test data, NOT test logic |

## §escalate (always handoff, never auto-fix)

The opposite list — these are always escalation, even if they look small:

| Category | Why |
|---|---|
| **Scaffold-layer change** | TopBar, navigation, base class, theme — affects every page |
| **Shared-rules / docs-hub edit** | Coordinator-owned |
| **Cross-frontend logic mirror** | "iOS has the same bug" — coordinator decides if/when to mirror |
| **Public API surface** | Adding / renaming / removing a public function or type |
| **Data source / endpoint URL** | Backend / config / feature flag changes |
| **Build / dependency version bump** | Gradle / pubspec / Package.swift / oh-package.json5 |
| **Migration script / DB schema** | One-way; coordinator owns rollout plan |
| **Removing existing code** | Even "obvious dead code" — see "Chesterton's fence" |
| **Three+ unrelated files** | "Sweep" changes; coordinator decides scope |

## §project-sensitive (project-specific override list)

This section is **filled by `/sulde-add-sensitive-file`**. Files listed here are explicitly marked sensitive: even if a change would otherwise fall in §allowlist, touching one of these triggers escalation.

```yaml
# Example (run /sulde-add-sensitive-file to populate)
sensitive_files:
  # - core-ui/src/main/java/.../AppRouter.kt
  # - Sources/CoreUI/AppRouter.swift
```

(Reads from `.sulde-config.yaml: scope_sensitivity.sensitive_files[]` — keep that and this list in sync.)

## How the rule shows up in handoffs

The handoff's `§ 改动文件清单` distinguishes the main change from self-fixes:

```markdown
## § 改动文件清单

### Main task changes (per task-md)
- `feature-foo/src/.../Foo.kt:42-68` — main logic
- `feature-foo/src/.../FooView.kt:14` — usage update

### Self-fixes (§allowlist)
- `feature-foo/src/.../Foo.kt:8` — removed unused import (already touching this file)
- `feature-foo/src/.../FooTest.kt:24` — updated test fixture (API signature changed in main task)
```

If self-fixes touch files **not in §allowlist**, the dev must NOT include them in the commit — they go in `§ escalation 候选` and the coordinator decides.

## Anti-pattern this prevents

A common failure mode: dev finishes a 1-hour task with a 50-line commit, but quietly adds a 200-line "while I was in there" cleanup. Coordinator now has a 250-line commit to review across two unrelated changes, and CI breaks for the cleanup half. The boundary keeps commits coherent + review attention focused.

See `<docs-hub>/ADR/0002-scaffold-bypass.md` for a related anti-pattern.
